  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Women and men workers and enterprises benefit from safe and healthy workplaces</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Under India&#x2019;s Decent Work Country Programme (DWCP) 2023-2027, Occupational Safety and Health (OSH) is a key priority for the government and social partners, building on previous efforts to advance national OSH policies and align with ILO standards. While India has ratified OSH Conventions 115 and 174, fundamental Conventions 155 and 187 remain pending. The inclusion of OSH in the Fundamental Principles and Rights at Work (FPRW) in 2022 has renewed momentum for ratification, with ongoing gap analyses and discussions initiated by the Ministry of Labour and Employment (MOLE). The ILO recommends India consider ratifying updated instruments, including C155, C187, and others, to strengthen its regulatory framework.
The ILO&#x2019;s CPO strategy for 2024-2025 focuses on supporting MOLE in developing a comprehensive National OSH Programme, enhancing state-level initiatives, expanding protections to informal workers, and improving reporting systems for occupational injuries and diseases. Technical assistance, awareness-raising, and capacity building for tripartite constituents will be central, including promoting dialogue on ratifying key OSH Conventions and addressing workplace violence and harassment across supply chains. These efforts contribute to Policy Outcome 6 on ensuring safe and healthy work environments, aligned with RBSA/DC projects and other CPO initiatives. Strong partnerships with government bodies, employers, workers, UN agencies, development partners, and ILO specialists will drive progress towards a safer, healthier working environment, supporting India&#x2019;s broader socio-economic development goals.</narrative>
  </description>
